+The prediction record (forecasters, quantities, paths by year, stated assumptions) was committed before any CRTC outcome series was downloaded. I then re-read the report's two paths from the data labels on its charts, checking every value against the totals in its text, and ran a design analysis by fake-data simulation before opening any CRTC outcome file \citep{gelman2014types, gelman2020workflow}. It asked, for each forecast quantity, whether the outcome could separate a world in which the report's impact occurred from one in which it didn't, given that the baseline itself could be wrong. For both revenue series it separated the two worlds in most simulations; for CPE it didn't, so CPE was set aside in advance as descriptive (appendix~\ref{app:methods}).
 
 Allowing for a wrong baseline is the hard part. In retrospective analysis of regulation, \enquote{developing a credible counterfactual is the most demanding aspect}, and the baselines assumed beforehand \enquote{must be revisited} to see whether outside factors changed the result \citep[286]{morgenstern2018retrospective}. Here the counterfactual is the report's own baseline, itself a forecast of how television would have fared under streaming without the reforms. So the check treats the baseline's error as unknown and reports results across a range of assumed scales for it. \textcite{kane2026sffa} varies the baseline period against which forecasts are judged; here the baseline is the report's own, and what varies is how wrong it's assumed to be.
 
