+The report's main input is uptake, the share of subscribers taking an entry-level package with chosen channels. The main findings are these:
+\begin{itemize}
+\item In the comparisons the plan fixed in advance, allowing for error in the report's own baseline at the scale the plan designated for each series, specialty and pay revenue, on which most of the job figure depends, stayed above the report's baseline and lost less ground against it than any of the outside uptake estimates the report cited (\lttvCitedUptakeLow{} to \lttvCitedUptakeHigh{}) implies. Distributors' revenue was consistent with the forecast. Under the largest error scales that alternative calibrations give, both verdicts become inconclusive.
+\item The report assumed \lttvByopSixteen{} uptake for 2016, far above the \lttvUptakeJuneShare{} of subscribers the CRTC counted on the entry-level package in mid-2016. A 2017 survey, outside the sources the analysis plan accepted, suggests starter-package adoption later came close to the report's \lttvByopSeventeen{} for 2017.
+\item In partial recalculations of the report's chain from uptake to revenue, added after the outcome data was opened, only a low uptake path among those tested is compatible with both revenue estimates at the designated scales.
+\item The testimony called an economy-wide employment estimate media jobs, presented the report's share of projected 2020 spending on Canadian programming as a share of current spending, and omitted the uptake assumption. The report's own totals put that share at \lttvCPEShareTwenty{}, not its stated 18\%.
+\end{itemize}
 
-Economists have long compared what was predicted before a regulation with what happened after it. \textcite[ii]{harrington1999accuracy} found that ex ante estimates of direct costs exceeded actual costs for 12 of 25 environmental and occupational-safety rules and fell short for 6, with the errors in quantities \enquote{driven by both baseline and compliance issues}, and a later set of case studies suggested \enquote{a slight tendency to overestimate both costs and benefits} \citep[285]{morgenstern2018retrospective}. \textcite{simpson2014overestimate} warns that a preponderance of overestimates need not show bias, since skewed cost distributions could produce one anyway; his review notes that the few studies of industry's own estimates found them above outcomes in most cases \citep[319--320]{simpson2014overestimate}.
+Economists have long compared what was predicted before a regulation with what happened after it. \textcite[297]{harrington2000accuracy} found that ex ante estimates of total direct costs exceeded actual costs for 14 of 28 environmental and occupational-safety rules and fell short for 3, with the quantity errors \enquote{driven both by difficulties in determining the baseline and by incomplete compliance}, and a later set of case studies suggested \enquote{a slight tendency to overestimate both costs and benefits} \citep[285]{morgenstern2018retrospective}. \textcite[322]{simpson2014overestimate} warns that a preponderance of overestimates need not show bias, since skewed cost distributions could produce one anyway; his review notes that the few studies of industry's own estimates found them above outcomes in most cases (pp.~319--321).
 
-That work concerns mainly regulators' estimates of compliance costs and benefits. This case is a different kind of forecast: an economy-wide estimate of jobs and output, made for stakeholder organizations and presented to legislators in testimony. In policy analysis generally, \enquote{Point predictions are common and expressions of uncertainty are rare} \citep[1]{manski2011certitude}.
+That work concerns mainly regulators' estimates of compliance costs and benefits. This case is a different kind of forecast: an economy-wide estimate of jobs and output, made for stakeholder organizations and presented to legislators in testimony. Such estimates have a critical literature of their own. \textcite[67]{crompton2006impact} documents how the job and output totals of commissioned economic-impact studies are inflated through multipliers and generous assumptions about which spending is new, and independent studies of sports facilities have found no significant effect on local development \citep[103]{siegfried2000sports}. This paper doesn't test the report's multipliers; it checks the predictions they were applied to. In policy analysis generally, \enquote{Point predictions are common and expressions of uncertainty are rare} \citep[1]{manski2011certitude}.
 
 A close precedent is \textcite{kane2026sffa}, who checked the admissions and enrolment forecasts made by universities and by Harvard's expert during the litigation that ended in the US Supreme Court's decision in \textit{Students for Fair Admissions v.\ Harvard}. He separates the expert's technical estimate from the forecasts the institutions put to the Court, and I make the same separation. The report's technical claim is a conditional forecast, which its authors offered as \enquote{the most plausible outlooks possible given available information} \citep[para.~iii]{nordicity2015television}: if the rules took effect and its assumptions held, revenue, programming spending and jobs would follow given paths. The public argument is what the committee was told.
 
